1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,660
One of China's biggest military
problems wasn't weapons.

2
00:00:04,300 --> 00:00:07,340
It was finding horses
strong enough to use them.

3
00:00:08,340 --> 00:00:12,820
In the second century BCE, the most
dangerous enemy of the Han dynasty

4
00:00:12,820 --> 00:00:18,600
lived on horseback: the Xiongnu,
nomads of the northern steppe.

5
00:00:19,400 --> 00:00:21,720
Emperor Wu had been
fighting them for decades,

6
00:00:21,720 --> 00:00:25,380
and to catch riders, he needed
better horses than his own.

7
00:00:25,740 --> 00:00:27,860
Then word came from the far west.

8
00:00:28,840 --> 00:00:32,980
More than two thousand miles
away, in the Ferghana Valley,

9
00:00:33,220 --> 00:00:36,240
lay a kingdom the Chinese called Dayuan.

10
00:00:37,140 --> 00:00:42,360
Its horses, people said, sweated blood,
and were descended from horses of heaven.

11
00:00:43,460 --> 00:00:46,620
The emperor sent an envoy
with a thousand pieces of

12
00:00:46,620 --> 00:00:50,220
gold and a horse made of
gold, to buy the best of them.

13
00:00:51,300 --> 00:00:53,480
Dayuan's nobles refused.

14
00:00:54,140 --> 00:00:58,020
According to the court historian Sima
Qian, they had done the arithmetic.

15
00:00:58,440 --> 00:01:02,080
China's envoys lost half their
men just crossing the deserts.

16
00:01:03,140 --> 00:01:05,300
How could it ever send an army?

17
00:01:05,980 --> 00:01:09,920
The envoy lost his temper, smashed
the golden horse, and left.

18
00:01:10,540 --> 00:01:13,820
At Dayuan's border, he
was ambushed and killed.

19
00:01:14,220 --> 00:01:19,580
In 104 BCE, the emperor sent
an army under Li Guangli,

20
00:01:19,600 --> 00:01:22,080
the brother of his favourite consort.

21
00:01:22,320 --> 00:01:24,647
His title was also his order:

22
00:01:24,647 --> 00:01:29,780
General of Ershi — the city
where the best horses were kept.

23
00:01:30,700 --> 00:01:35,620
The oasis kingdoms along the road shut
their gates and refused to feed his men.

24
00:01:36,520 --> 00:01:41,060
By the time the army reached Dayuan, it
was a few thousand starving soldiers,

25
00:01:41,060 --> 00:01:45,620
and it was beaten at the same border
town where the envoy had died.

26
00:01:46,600 --> 00:01:48,220
Two years after setting out,

27
00:01:48,220 --> 00:01:51,820
Li Guangli was back at the frontier
with one or two men in ten.

28
00:01:51,920 --> 00:01:53,740
He asked to bring them home.

29
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:58,440
The emperor shut the Jade Gate,
the pass into China, with an order:

30
00:01:59,200 --> 00:02:02,360
any soldier who tried to come
through would be beheaded.

31
00:02:02,760 --> 00:02:05,040
His ministers begged him to give up.

32
00:02:05,040 --> 00:02:06,200
He refused.

33
00:02:06,820 --> 00:02:10,140
If China could not beat one
small kingdom, he reasoned,

34
00:02:10,140 --> 00:02:13,620
it would be the laughing
stock of every foreign land.

35
00:02:13,660 --> 00:02:17,780
He had the loudest critics
prosecuted, and started again.

36
00:02:18,560 --> 00:02:25,820
In 102 BCE, sixty thousand soldiers
marched west, with a hundred thousand oxen,

37
00:02:25,820 --> 00:02:30,820
more than thirty thousand horses, and
donkeys and camels by the tens of thousands.

38
00:02:32,040 --> 00:02:35,220
The whole empire, Sima Qian
wrote, was in upheaval.

39
00:02:35,400 --> 00:02:38,040
The army even brought two
experts in judging horses,

40
00:02:38,040 --> 00:02:40,820
to choose the best ones
when the city fell.

41
00:02:41,480 --> 00:02:44,900
Thirty thousand men reached
the capital of Dayuan.

42
00:02:44,900 --> 00:02:47,220
The city had no wells.

43
00:02:47,220 --> 00:02:50,340
It drew its water from a
river outside the walls,

44
00:02:50,340 --> 00:02:53,420
so the Han brought in
engineers, and moved the river.

45
00:02:54,460 --> 00:02:57,660
After more than forty
days, the outer wall fell.

46
00:02:58,720 --> 00:03:04,100
Inside, the nobles decided who
was to blame: their own king,

47
00:03:04,100 --> 00:03:07,140
who had hidden the horses
and killed the envoy.

48
00:03:07,520 --> 00:03:10,420
They killed him, and sent
his head out with an offer.

49
00:03:11,220 --> 00:03:14,240
Stop the attack, and take
your pick of our horses.

50
00:03:14,580 --> 00:03:20,740
Refuse, and we kill every good horse in the
city — and an allied army is on its way.

51
00:03:20,740 --> 00:03:23,480
Li Guangli took it.

52
00:03:23,480 --> 00:03:27,080
His experts chose several dozen
of the finest horses and more

53
00:03:27,080 --> 00:03:31,960
than three thousand ordinary ones,
and the army turned for home.

54
00:03:32,560 --> 00:03:36,940
Of the sixty thousand who had
marched out, just over ten thousand

55
00:03:36,940 --> 00:03:42,120
came back through the Jade Gate,
with just over a thousand horses.

56
00:03:43,160 --> 00:03:46,660
Sima Qian was blunt about
why so many had died.

57
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:48,540
Not hunger, and not battle.

58
00:03:48,540 --> 00:03:51,040
Officers who stole from their own men.

59
00:03:51,680 --> 00:03:56,880
A year later, Dayuan's nobles killed the
king the Chinese had put on their throne.

60
00:03:57,520 --> 00:04:00,620
The new king sent his son to
the Han court as a hostage,

61
00:04:00,620 --> 00:04:04,340
and agreed to send two heavenly
horses east every year.

62
00:04:04,600 --> 00:04:09,600
It had begun with a golden horse,
offered to a kingdom that would not sell.

63
00:04:09,860 --> 00:04:13,580
It ended with two living horses a year.

64
00:04:13,960 --> 00:04:17,500
And around the emperor's palaces,
as far as the eye could see,

65
00:04:17,500 --> 00:04:23,180
grew fields of alfalfa from Ferghana
seed: fodder, for the horses of heaven.

66
00:04:24,380 --> 00:04:26,631
Empires have gone to war for gold,

67
00:04:26,631 --> 00:04:30,760
for land and for revenge —
and at least once, for horses.

68
00:04:31,560 --> 00:04:35,280
If you want more forgotten wars and
strange turning points like this,

69
00:04:35,280 --> 00:04:37,780
leave a like and subscribe
to Animated Tales.

70
00:04:37,780 --> 00:04:39,700
Until next time.
